WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.379 --> 00:00:01.212
- Ew!

00:00:01.212 --> 00:00:03.019
Wow!

00:00:03.019 --> 00:00:05.602
(upbeat music)

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:10.790
One thing you can buy off
Ebay is a total stranger's

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:12.340
school yearbook.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.370
They usually cost around
ten to 30 dollars.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:15.970
So they're not super expensive.

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:18.320
I bought yearbooks from
five different decades,

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.520
the 90s, 80, 50s, 40s and 30s.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.770
If you buy somebody's old yearbook,

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.590
it comes with all the messages

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.540
that their friends wrote to them.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.680
The most popular thing
people wrote in yearbooks

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:29.810
when I was in school was,

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.330
hope you have a good summer.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.160
We're gonna see what people of the past

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.390
wrote in their yearbooks,

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:38.000
and what kinda people
owned these yearbooks.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.369
My first yearbook is from 1993.

00:00:40.369 --> 00:00:42.160
(popping)

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.050
It's from Florida.

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:46.430
The Ebay listing said that it had lots of

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:47.740
nasty writing in it.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.420
Lots of obnoxious comments,

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:51.590
and that's exactly what I wanted.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:54.320
I see devil horns drawn on a teacher.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.880
I'm noticing a lot of the
handwriting is the same.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.240
I think the person who owned this yearbook

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:02.277
wrote messages to himself.

00:01:02.277 --> 00:01:04.637
"Dear me, you are the
greatest looking guy.

00:01:04.637 --> 00:01:06.937
"I think you are the greatest
looking guy in the world.

00:01:06.937 --> 00:01:09.257
"Love yourself."

00:01:09.257 --> 00:01:11.227
"You are so cute.

00:01:11.227 --> 00:01:13.151
"Love yourself."

00:01:13.151 --> 00:01:15.620
I mean, I guess it's nice to...

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.410
I can't even, it's too sad.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:23.200
Okay, I found some real
signatures from other people.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.107
One of them says, "You
are the most ugliest,

00:01:25.107 --> 00:01:28.640
"most pain in the butt cool guy I know."

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:29.473
Okay.

00:01:29.473 --> 00:01:30.306
"Stay cool.

00:01:30.306 --> 00:01:31.160
"Love always."

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.170
I feel a little bit bad for this guy.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.280
Not a lot of friends
wrote in this yearbook,

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.070
which I can relate to.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:39.410
I remember just having
like a couple close friends

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:40.330
sign my yearbook.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.570
And then having just a lot of space left.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.600
Which is totally fine.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:44.950
But I definitely remember that feeling

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:45.783
where you're like,

00:01:45.783 --> 00:01:48.490
wow, my yearbook should be covered in like

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.840
messages and inside jokes
from all my many friends.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:52.940
Oh, wait.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:54.280
I don't have any.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.283
This yearbook is from 1982
and it's from Georgia.

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:01.010
(freestyle music)

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:02.817
These people wrote a lot of messages.

00:02:02.817 --> 00:02:04.837
"I'm so glad we've got
class together this year.

00:02:04.837 --> 00:02:05.947
"I love you to death,

00:02:05.947 --> 00:02:07.587
"cuz you're a really super guy."

00:02:07.587 --> 00:02:10.047
"You're a very nice
person and I know for sure

00:02:10.047 --> 00:02:11.680
"nothing will ever change that."

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.267
How can you be so sure?

00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:14.710
"Love ya, Haley."

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.097
Look, Haley, people change, okay?

00:02:17.097 --> 00:02:19.517
"You are one of the nicest guys I know.

00:02:19.517 --> 00:02:20.990
"Call me sometime."

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:24.487
This is a whole page written by someone!

00:02:24.487 --> 00:02:25.617
"Where do I begin?

00:02:25.617 --> 00:02:27.247
"You've meant so much to me.

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:28.387
"I love you dearly.

00:02:28.387 --> 00:02:30.967
"When you make that first
million, you ought to drop by

00:02:30.967 --> 00:02:32.402
"and pay me a visit."

00:02:32.402 --> 00:02:35.047
Okay, now this just seems
like fishing for money.

00:02:35.047 --> 00:02:36.317
"You know I love you even though

00:02:36.317 --> 00:02:37.987
"I do pick on you all the time.

00:02:37.987 --> 00:02:41.650
"And if you didn't know, you do now."

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:44.070
These people are so friendly.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:45.610
If not a little fake.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.830
Almost all of the
messages for this 80s guy

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.404
are about how nice he is,

00:02:50.404 --> 00:02:52.640
how good a friend he is.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.056
But then there's just one message

00:02:55.056 --> 00:02:58.507
that really makes me doubt all of that.

00:02:58.507 --> 00:02:59.847
"Hey, how ya been?

00:02:59.847 --> 00:03:01.347
"I haven't seen you much.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:02.219
"I'll never forget the
time you poured Coke "

00:03:02.219 --> 00:03:04.517
down my bathing suit.

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:05.727
"And ice.

00:03:05.727 --> 00:03:08.477
"And tied my hands
together at Shane's party.

00:03:08.477 --> 00:03:10.817
"You are really a sweet guy.

00:03:10.817 --> 00:03:12.337
"Good luck always."

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:15.483
What?

00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:18.180
And I get, sort of like
having a food fight.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:23.180
The tying of the hands,
that's what really make me

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.540
question how nice this guy is.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:26.430
But, okay.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.530
Our next decade is the 50s.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.750
This yearbook is from 1958,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.480
from someplace in Massachusetts.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.897
(rock-and-roll music)

00:03:34.897 --> 00:03:35.857
"To Joe.

00:03:35.857 --> 00:03:39.420
"The kid who always had to
have a smoke in Driver's Ed."

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.530
So apparently Joe's nickname was Smokey.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.487
Possibly because he smoked.

00:03:43.487 --> 00:03:46.477
"If anyone has a sense of humor, it's you.

00:03:46.477 --> 00:03:47.877
"You're a doll."

00:03:47.877 --> 00:03:50.997
"To Joe, that wonder boy with the girls.

00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:53.867
"And who caused Mr.
Wiggin to have ulcers."

00:03:53.867 --> 00:03:56.897
"To Joe, the boy who
always bothers me in class.

00:03:56.897 --> 00:03:58.727
"Between his jokes and actions,

00:03:58.727 --> 00:04:01.030
"he is one boy to watch out for."

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:02.487
A boy to watch out for.

00:04:02.487 --> 00:04:05.280
"Joe, a boy who really
knows how to tease."

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.587
This guy, from the 50s,
was really good at math.

00:04:07.587 --> 00:04:10.847
"The brightest, most intelligent
genius math student."

00:04:10.847 --> 00:04:13.397
"To Joe, the smartest math student."

00:04:13.397 --> 00:04:16.300
"To Joe, a kid who always
did his math homework

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:17.560
so I could copy it."

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.090
I know copying is wrong,

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:21.460
but I feel like there's
a certain selflessness,

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:24.160
of just being like, yeah,
the homework's easy for me.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:25.200
You can copy it.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.033
I don't care.

00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:29.290
I respect that, even though it's wrong.

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:31.630
Honestly, the guy who owned this yearbook

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:33.380
seems kind of cool.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:35.496
This yearbook is from 1947 in New York.

00:04:35.496 --> 00:04:37.890
(swing music)

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.667
A girl owned this yearbook.

00:04:39.667 --> 00:04:40.867
"Best of luck always."

00:04:40.867 --> 00:04:41.700
"Lots of luck."

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:42.533
"Lots of luck."

00:04:42.533 --> 00:04:43.366
"Best of luck."

00:04:43.366 --> 00:04:44.199
"Good luck."

00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:45.032
"Lots of luck."

00:04:45.032 --> 00:04:45.865
"Lots of luck."

00:04:45.865 --> 00:04:46.698
"Best of luck."

00:04:46.698 --> 00:04:47.531
"Lots of luck."

00:04:47.531 --> 00:04:48.817
"Lots of luck to you always."

00:04:48.817 --> 00:04:50.727
"Sincere wishes and lots of luck."

00:04:50.727 --> 00:04:51.560
"Best wishes."

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:52.457
"Best luck."

00:04:52.457 --> 00:04:53.627
"To a swell girl."

00:04:53.627 --> 00:04:54.897
"To a swell girl."

00:04:54.897 --> 00:04:56.177
"To a swell girl."

00:04:56.177 --> 00:04:57.727
"To a very swell girl."

00:04:57.727 --> 00:05:00.027
"Good luck and best wishes."

00:05:00.027 --> 00:05:01.280
"To a swell girl."

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:02.800
A lof of people signed this yearbook.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.140
They either say, good luck,
or you're a swell girl.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.400
Or good luck to a swell girl.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.323
Maybe the girl who had this book just was,

00:05:11.323 --> 00:05:15.010
was that swell, and needed that much luck.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:17.073
The last yearbook is from 1935.

00:05:17.073 --> 00:05:19.260
`(swing music)

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:21.960
It's crazy that this yearbook
is more than 80 years old.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.930
A young man owned this book in Illinois.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:25.840
There's a section that says,

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.890
athletics, and there's just a tractor.

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:30.810
I mean I'm from the
Midwest, but I know that

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:32.470
a tractor is not a sport.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:34.030
There's lots of signatures in here,

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:37.910
but people just signed their names.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:39.040
Not even a good luck.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.020
Not even to a swell boy.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:42.180
This is just a bunch of names.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:46.100
So people didn't really
write personal messages

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:50.010
in this 1935 yearbook from Illinois.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.410
There's something sort of
desperate about a yearbook,

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.690
like these are the special
times you must remember!

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:57.300
And then you look
through it and it's like,

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.170
these pictures don't really reflect

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:01.280
any actual thing that happened.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:04.060
You're supposed to write
something meaningful

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.730
for your friend in their yearbook.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:07.170
And you're just like, what do I write?

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:08.040
I don't know.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:09.339
Stay cool?

00:06:09.339 --> 00:06:11.190
You're a swell girl?

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:12.060
Best of luck?

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:13.750
Like there's nothing to say.

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:15.620
It's kind of like signing a greeting card.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:17.560
You're just like ahhhh.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.250
It doesn't matter what decade it is.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.750
No one ever knows what
you're supposed to write

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:22.583
in a yearbook.

00:06:22.583 --> 00:06:24.010
So, that being said, please,

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:25.510
I hope you have a good summer.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:30.955
Have a good summer and stay cool.

00:06:30.955 --> 00:06:34.122
(slow carousel music)

